:: day-2_gardens [nobr]
<div id="ash-banner" class="scene-image"><<sceneImage "day_minus_2/royal_gardens_ash_male" "Royal Gardens">></div>

<h2>Royal Gardens   Ash's Arrival</h2>

<p>The gardens at dusk are the only place in Crown's Rest that doesn't feel like a cage.</p>

<p>Stone paths wind between flower beds   night-blooming jasmine opening its white throats to the darkening sky, winter roses still holding colour despite the season, herbs planted by some long-dead queen who believed a monarch should know what grew in her own soil. Hedgerows trimmed into geometric shapes border the paths. A fountain at the center   a silver stag pouring water from its antlers   catches the last copper light and scatters it.</p>

<p>I sit on a stone bench near the fountain. The evening air smells of jasmine and woodsmoke from the kitchens. My head is full of books and blood tests and the phrase <em>in totality</em> and the look in my mother's eyes when she said <em>everything your great-grandmother bled for.</em></p>

<p>I'm staring at nothing when I hear lute strings.</p>

<p>Not court music   something looser, rougher, with the slightly irregular rhythm of someone playing for themselves rather than an audience. The melody wanders, finds something beautiful, loses it, circles back. It sounds like a conversation between the musician and the instrument, private and unguarded.</p>

<p>I know that style. I'd know it through a wall, through a storm, through ten years of silence.</p>

<div id="ash-select" class="inline-choice">
<<link "A young man steps around the hedgerow">>
<<set $bardGender to "male">>
<<set $bardPronoun to "he">>
<<set $bardPronounObj to "him">>
<<set $bardPronounPos to "his">>
<<set $bardPronounCap to "He">>
<<set $bardPronounPosCap to "His">>
<<set $bardPortraitSlug to "ash_male">>
<<replace "#ash-banner"><<sceneImage "day_minus_2/royal_gardens_ash_male" "Royal Gardens">><</replace>>
<<replace "#ash-select">><div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Ash."</div>

<p><<npcPortrait $bardPortraitSlug "Ash">>He steps around the hedgerow, lute slung across his body, half-smile already in place. Ash Ashwood   half-elf, traveling bard, the closest thing I have to a friend who doesn't call me "Your Highness" and mean it.</p>

<p>He looks the same. Different. Both. His hair is longer   dark with that faint copper undertone that catches light in ways human hair doesn't, swept back from pointed ears and falling past his shoulders. Eyes the colour of honey, tilted slightly, always looking like he knows something amusing that he's deciding whether to share. Lean and angular, dressed in travel-worn leather and a shirt that might have been white several washes ago, boots dusty from the road.</p><</replace>>
<</link>>

<<link "A young woman steps around the hedgerow">>
<<set $bardGender to "female">>
<<set $bardPronoun to "she">>
<<set $bardPronounObj to "her">>
<<set $bardPronounPos to "her">>
<<set $bardPronounCap to "She">>
<<set $bardPronounPosCap to "Her">>
<<set $bardPortraitSlug to "ash_female">>
<<replace "#ash-banner"><<sceneImage "day_minus_2/royal_gardens_ash_female" "Royal Gardens">><</replace>>
<<replace "#ash-select">><div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Ash."</div>

<p><<npcPortrait $bardPortraitSlug "Ash">>She steps around the hedgerow, lute slung across her body, half-smile already in place. Ash Ashwood   half-elf, traveling bard, the closest thing I have to a friend who doesn't call me "Your Highness" and mean it.</p>

<p>She looks the same. Different. Both. Her hair is longer   dark with that faint copper undertone that catches light in ways human hair doesn't, swept back from pointed ears and falling past her shoulders. Eyes the colour of honey, tilted slightly, always looking like she knows something amusing that she's deciding whether to share. Lean and angular, dressed in travel-worn leather and a shirt that might have been white several washes ago, boots dusty from the road.</p><</replace>>
<</link>>
</div>

<p>We met when I was fifteen. Tournament day. Ash was busking for coins near the festival stalls, playing a song so filthy that three matrons walked away and two came closer. I laughed so hard I choked on candied apple. We talked for four hours. We've been writing letters   erratically, across distances that shouldn't allow friendship but somehow do   ever since.</p>

<<if $bardGender is "female">>
<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"You look like someone deciding which cliff to jump off." She settles onto the bench beside me, adjusting the lute across her lap.</div>
<<else>>
<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"You look like a man deciding which cliff to jump off." He settles onto the bench beside me, adjusting the lute across his lap.</div>
<</if>>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"That's closer to accurate than you think."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"Ah."</div>

<p>Ash doesn't push. That's the thing about <<print $bardPronounObj>>   <<print $bardPronoun>> has the patience of an immortal, which <<print $bardPronoun>>'s technically halfway to being, and the emotional intelligence of someone who has spent <<print $bardPronounPos>> life reading rooms for a living. <<print $bardPronounCap>> sets <<print $bardPronounPos>> fingers on the strings and plays something soft. Not a performance. A presence.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"I thought you were in Tormark," I say, because saying anything else would mean saying the real thing, and I'm not ready.</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"I was. Terrible audiences. The Tormarkians appreciate a twelve-verse ballad about agricultural policy but look at you blankly if you play something with actual emotion. I left before I died of boredom." <<print $bardPronounCap>> glances at me sideways. "I got a letter. From your mother, actually. She said you might need a friend."</div>

<p>Of course she did. Of course Mother   even from her sickbed, even while dying, even while planning the demolition and reconstruction of her only child's entire existence   thought to summon the one person who might make the demolition bearable.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"She's thorough."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"She's your mother. It's the same thing."</div>

<p>We sit. Ash plays. The melody shifts   something older now, a song I half-recognize from childhood. The jasmine exhales its sweetness into the cooling air. Above us, the first stars appear   the real ones, not the painted kind on library ceilings.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"Whatever it is," Ash says, not looking at me, fingers still moving on the strings, "you've never been a coward, Edrin. You're the bravest person I know. Not because you're fearless, but because you're terrified of everything and do it anyway."</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"What if 'doing it' means losing... myself?"</div>

<p><<print $bardPronounCap>> stops playing. The silence is sudden and specific   the kind that happens when someone puts down an instrument after playing for a long time, and the air hasn't yet figured out how to fill the space.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"I've been remaking myself since I was born." Ash's voice is quiet. "Half of two worlds, belonging fully to neither. Every town I enter, I decide which version of myself to wear. It's not losing yourself. It's finding out which parts were real all along."</div>

<p>The words land in a place I didn't know was open. Something behind my sternum shifts   not understanding, exactly, but the premonition of understanding. The shadow of a thought I'm not ready to have.</p>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">EDRIN</span>"Stay," I say. "For a few days. I might need..." I don't finish.</div>

<div class="dialogue"><span class="speaker">ASH</span>"I'm here." Ash picks up the lute again. "I'm not going anywhere."</div>

<p><<print $bardPronounCap>> plays until the stars are thick and the fountain's splash is the only other sound. I don't tell <<print $bardPronounObj>> about the Rite. Not tonight. Tonight I just need someone beside me who doesn't want anything, who isn't angling for position or advantage, who plays music in the garden because the evening is beautiful and that's enough.</p>

<p>It's not enough. But it helps.</p>

